rationale:
The reviewed tests cover each obligation in `requirements.sdk.habeas.evidence_conversion.legacy_freshness`. I judged the tests against the requirement text and did not request implementation source.

- **Missing version loads as `*`.** `src/evidence_format.rs#mod[tests]/fn[legacy_conversion_preserves_every_historical_field_without_resolving_sources]` decodes legacy records for Definition, Citation and Site subjects and asserts `record.revision == "*"`. It also asserts that the output and dependencies are preserved and that `encode` writes `review_version == "*"`. `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[every_review_kind_loads_both_formats_and_reports_each_application_failure_once]` asserts that `load_evidence` returns revision `*` for a legacy record of every review kind.
- **In-place conversion keeps the wildcard.** `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[current_legacy_authoring_converts_without_configuration_and_stdout_never_converts]` runs `author_evidence_with_options` on a legacy file. It asserts the written file has `review_version == "*"` and that `view` of it equals the old record. It also asserts that stdout output never converts or modifies the file.
- **The wildcard matches any requested revision.** `src/evidence_format.rs#mod[tests]/fn[native_freshness_checks_versions_inputs_and_application_fields]` calls `record_is_current` on a `*` record with `"future-review-version"` and `"any"`. It also checks that a concrete but different revision is not current, so only the full `*` value acts as a wildcard.
- **Inputs and application checks stay intact under `*`.** In the same native freshness test, a changed requirement text makes the `*` record not current. In `every_review_kind_loads_both_formats_and_reports_each_application_failure_once`, legacy and native `*` records produce no findings when unchanged. The test then applies one mutation at a time, and each must yield the expected finding, identical for legacy and native forms:
  - determination false;
  - changed input;
  - changed subject text;
  - changed reference set;
  - changed citations and changed file or item;
  - changed site requirement, queries and fixmes;
  - `applies` false.

  Stale records still load, because staleness is an application finding rather than a loading error.
- **New assessments record a concrete version.** `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[authoring_keeps_the_prepared_subject_snapshot_and_concrete_version]` asserts the produced `review_version` equals `REVIEW_VERSION` and is not `*`.

I found no untested obligation and no missing scenario that could expose different required behavior. There are no `{fixme}` acknowledgments, and none are needed.
